<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Applying multiple probability laws to independent CBE project milestones</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A Grade 11 learner is undertaking two independent practical tasks for their school competency portfolio: an Agri-nutrition project (Event \(A\)) and a Digital Enterprise project (Event \(B\)). Based on formative assessment criteria, the probability that the learner successfully achieves the required performance benchmark for the Agri-nutrition project is <b>{@str_A@}</b>, and the probability for the Digital Enterprise project is <b>{@str_B@}</b>.</p>

<p><b>a) </b> Find the probability that the learner successfully achieves the competency benchmark for <b>both</b> projects.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as an exact decimal).</i></p>
<p>\(P(A \cap B) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Find the probability that the learner successfully achieves the competency benchmark for <b>at least one</b> of the two projects.</p>
<p>\(P(A \cup B) =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Find the probability that the learner successfully achieves the benchmark for <b>exactly one</b> project (either agri-nutrition only, or digital enterprise only).</p>
<p>\(P(\text{exactly one}) =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part a: Achieving Both Benchmarks</b><br>
Since the projects are independent competency tasks, we use the multiplication rule for independent events:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \cap B) = P(A) \times P(B) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \cap B) = {@str_A@} \times {@str_B@} = {@ta1_str@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(A \cap B) = {@ta1_str@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: Achieving At Least One Benchmark</b><br>
"At least one" represents the union of the two competency achievements. We apply the general addition rule:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(A \cup B) = {@str_A@} + {@str_B@} - {@ta1_str@} = {@ta2_str@} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(A \cup B) = {@ta2_str@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part c: Achieving Exactly One Benchmark</b><br>
Achieving exactly one means meeting the standard for at least one (\(A \cup B\)) but NOT meeting both simultaneously (\(A \cap B\)). Therefore, we subtract the intersection from the union:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{exactly one}) = P(A \cup B) - P(A \cap B) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(\text{exactly one}) = {@ta2_str@} - {@ta1_str@} = {@ta3_str@} \)</p>
<p><i>(Alternatively: \(P(A \cap B') + P(A' \cap B) = ({@str_A@} \times {@comp_B_str@}) + ({@comp_A_str@} \times {@str_B@}) = {@ta3_str@}\)).</i></p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(\text{exactly one}) = {@ta3_str@} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
